// result_end

\caption(Relative residual against products with \(A\) or \(A^\top\) for the PageRank system at \(\alpha=0.85\). BiCG uses a random shadow vector. The black line is the tolerance \(10^{-10}\).)
\alttext(Line chart of log10 relative residual against matrix products, from 0 to 185. Power iteration falls in a straight line, reaching minus 10 at 146 products. GMRES falls smoothly and reaches minus 10 at 36. BiCGSTAB from a random start follows close to GMRES and reaches it at 40. BiCG with a random shadow zig-zags downward and reaches it at 78. BiCGSTAB from zero is erratic, with a large jump up after 70 products, and reaches minus 10 only at 180.)

All the converged runs agree with the reference `page_rank` to \(6\times10^{-13}\) or better, and their recursively updated residuals agree with the true residuals \(\|b-Ax\|/\|b\|\). The ranking follows the theory:

- **BiCG with \(\tilde r_0=b\)** stops after one step with a breakdown, exactly as derived above. Its iterate is still \(x_1=b/(1-\alpha)\), which has the wrong scale and a residual of 3.3.
- **BiCG with a random shadow** converges in 39 steps (78 products), about half the work of power iteration. Its residual is not monotone: BiCG minimises nothing, and the peaks are steps where \(\tilde r_k^\top r_k\) happened to be small.
- **GMRES** is the benchmark, because it minimises \(\|r_k\|\) over the same Krylov space: 36 products, but it stores all 36 basis vectors and the orthogonalisation cost grows with \(k^2\).
- **BiCGSTAB from \(x_0=0\)** has \(\tilde r_0=r_0=b\), the bad shadow. It does not break down outright, since its smoothing step moves \(r_k\) away from the eigenvector, but it needs 180 products with large jumps. From a random probability vector it needs 40, close to GMRES, and with constant memory.

> As damping approaches one

The interesting regime is \(\alpha\to1\), where power iteration needs about \(1/(1-\alpha)\) times longer, and
the near-unit eigenvalues of \(P\) put eigenvalues of \(A\) close to zero. We count the products
needed to reach \(10^{-10}\) for \(\alpha\) from 0.5 to 0.999.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bicg_damping.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <random>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits();
  const std::size_t n = digits.nrows();
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> x(n, std::vector<double>(64));
  for (int j = 0; j < 64; ++j) {
    const auto column = digits.get_column("pixel_" + std::to_string(j)).values();
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) x[i][j] = column[i];
  }
  const auto lists = dm::geometry::knn_lists(x, 10);
  std::vector<std::pair<std::size_t, std::size_t>> edges;
  for (std::size_t u = 0; u < n; ++u)
    for (int v : lists[u]) edges.emplace_back(u, v);
  std::mt19937_64 rng(1976);
  std::uniform_real_distribution<double> u(0.5, 1.5);
  std::vector<double> shadow(n), x0(n);
  for (auto& v : shadow) v = u(rng);
  for (auto& v : x0) v = u(rng);
  const double x0_sum = std::accumulate(x0.begin(), x0.end(), 0.0);
  for (auto& v : x0) v /= x0_sum;
  const double tol = 1e-10;

  std::print("| \\(\\alpha\\) | power iteration | \\(\\lceil\\log\\varepsilon/\\log\\alpha\\rceil\\) | BiCG, random shadow | BiCGSTAB, \\(x_0=0\\) | BiCGSTAB, random \\(x_0\\) | GMRES |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<double> lx, lp, lb, ls, lg;
  for (double alpha : {0.5, 0.85, 0.95, 0.99, 0.999}) {
    const auto sys = alg::page_rank_system(n, edges, alpha);
    const auto A = [&](const std::vector<double>& v) { return sys.matrix.spmv(v); };
    const auto At = [&](const std::vector<double>& v) { return sys.transpose.spmv(v); };
    const double bnorm = std::sqrt(std::inner_product(sys.rhs.begin(), sys.rhs.end(), sys.rhs.begin(), 0.0));
    std::vector<double> v(n, 0.0);
    int power = 0;
    for (; power < 200000; ++power) {
      const auto Av = A(v);
      double res = 0;
      for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) res += (sys.rhs[i] - Av[i]) * (sys.rhs[i] - Av[i]);
      if (std::sqrt(res) / bnorm <= tol) break;
      for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) v[i] += sys.rhs[i] - Av[i];
    }
    const auto bicg = alg::biconjugate_gradient(A, At, sys.rhs, 5000, tol, shadow);
    dm::linalg::SolverOptions o;
    o.tol = tol;
    o.max_iter = 5000;
    const auto stab0 = dm::linalg::bicgstab(sys.matrix, sys.rhs, {}, o);
    const auto stab = dm::linalg::bicgstab(sys.matrix, sys.rhs, x0, o);
    const auto gm = dm::linalg::gmres(sys.matrix, sys.rhs, {}, o, 0);
    const auto cell = [](const auto& r, int per) {
      if (r.converged) return std::format("{}", per * static_cast<int>(r.iterations));
      return std::isfinite(static_cast<double>(r.residual_norm))
                 ? std::format("fails ({:.0e})", static_cast<double>(r.residual_norm))
                 : std::string("fails (NaN)");
    };
    std::print("| {} | {} | {:.0f} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", alpha, power, std::ceil(std::log(tol) / std::log(alpha)),
               bicg.converged ? std::format("{}", bicg.products) : "fails", cell(stab0, 2), cell(stab, 2), cell(gm, 1));
    lx.push_back(std::log10(1 / (1 - alpha)));
    lp.push_back(std::log10(power));
    lb.push_back(std::log10(bicg.products));
    ls.push_back(stab.converged ? std::log10(2.0 * stab.iterations) : std::nan(""));
    lg.push_back(std::log10(gm.iterations));
  }
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                  .lines(lx, lp, "power iteration", dm::RGB{148, 163, 184}, 2.5)
                  .points(lx, lp, "", dm::RGB{148, 163, 184}, 6.0)
                  .lines(lx, lb, "BiCG", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2.5)
                  .points(lx, lb, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 6.0)
                  .lines(lx, lg, "GMRES", dm::RGB{22, 163, 74}, 2.5)
                  .points(lx, lg, "", dm::RGB{22, 163, 74}, 6.0);
  std::vector<double> sx, sy;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < ls.size(); ++i)
    if (!std::isnan(ls[i])) sx.push_back(lx[i]), sy.push_back(ls[i]);
  plot.points(sx, sy, "BiCGSTAB", dm::RGB{217, 119, 6}, 8.0)
      .y_limits(0.8, 6)
      .title("Work to reach 1e-10 against 1 / (1 - alpha)")
      .x_label("log10 1 / (1 - alpha)")
      .y_label("log10 products with A or its transpose")
      .size(820, 460)
      .save_svg("build/bicg_damping.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| \(\alpha\) | power iteration | \(\lceil\log\varepsilon/\log\alpha\rceil\) | BiCG, random shadow | BiCGSTAB, \(x_0=0\) | BiCGSTAB, random \(x_0\) | GMRES |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0.5 | 34 | 34 | 34 | 24 | 20 | 16 |
| 0.85 | 146 | 142 | 76 | 180 | 38 | 36 |
| 0.95 | 464 | 449 | 140 | 1514 | 76 | 63 |
| 0.99 | 2381 | 2292 | 252 | fails (NaN) | 168 | 120 |
| 0.999 | 24028 | 23015 | 406 | fails (NaN) | 382 | 170 |
\image(figures/bicg_damping.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Products with \(A\) or \(A^\top\) needed to reach a relative residual of \(10^{-10}\) against \(1/(1-\alpha)\), on log scales. BiCG uses a random shadow vector and BiCGSTAB a random starting vector.)
\alttext(Log-log plot of products needed against 1 over 1 minus alpha, for alpha 0.5, 0.85, 0.95, 0.99 and 0.999. Power iteration rises with slope about 1, from 34 to 24,028. BiCG rises much more slowly, from 34 to 406. GMRES is lowest, from 16 to 170. BiCGSTAB from a random start lies between GMRES and BiCG, from 20 to 382.)

Power iteration tracks its bound \(\lceil\log\varepsilon/\log\alpha\rceil\) to within 5%, so its work grows like \(1/(1-\alpha)\): a thousand-fold increase from \(\alpha=0.5\) to \(0.999\) costs 700 times more products. The Krylov methods grow far more slowly, roughly as the square root of \(1/(1-\alpha)\): 12 times for BiCG and 11 times for GMRES over the same range, so at \(\alpha=0.999\) BiCG is 59 times cheaper than power iteration. The reason is the polynomial argument above. Pushing \(\alpha\) towards 1 moves the dozen outlying eigenvalues towards zero, but each still costs only about one Krylov step to remove, and the bulk barely moves.

BiCGSTAB from \(x_0=0\) is the warning. Its cost grows from 24 to 1,514 products by \(\alpha=0.95\), and at \(\alpha=0.99\) and \(0.999\) it produces NaN: with \(\tilde r_0\) along the eigenvector \(\mathbf 1\) of \(A^\top\), its inner products lose all information. Starting from a random probability vector, which changes \(r_0\) and so \(\tilde r_0\), it converges at every \(\alpha\) with work close to BiCG's.

> Summary

- BiCG is the Petrov–Galerkin method on \(\mathcal K_k(A,r_0)\) against \(\mathcal K_k(A^\top,\tilde r_0)\). Bi-orthogonality makes \(\tilde R^\top AR\) tridiagonal, which gives CG-like two-term recurrences at the price of a product with \(A^\top\) per step and no minimisation property.
- It can break down with \(A\) non-singular, either when \(\tilde p_k^\top Ap_k=0\) or when \(\tilde r_k^\top r_k=0\). On PageRank the default shadow \(\tilde r_0=b\) is a left eigenvector of \(A\), and BiCG breaks down at its first step, with \(\alpha_0=1/(1-\alpha)\) and \(\tilde r_1=0\) exactly. The same choice makes BiCGSTAB from zero fail as \(\alpha\to1\). A random shadow vector, or a random starting vector for BiCGSTAB, removes the problem.
- On the digit graph, power iteration needs about \(1/(1-\alpha)\) times more work as damping rises, while BiCG, BiCGSTAB and GMRES grow roughly with its square root, because only a dozen eigenvalues of \(A\) approach zero. GMRES needs the fewest products. BiCG and BiCGSTAB need about 2 to 2.4 times as many, with constant memory.
- `algorithms::biconjugate_gradient` is matrix-free, takes an optional shadow vector, and reports breakdowns instead of returning a silently wrong answer. `algorithms::page_rank_system` builds the sparse PageRank system and its transpose, and `geometry::knn_lists` builds the nearest-neighbour graph.

> References

- Lanczos, C. (1952). Solution of systems of linear equations by minimized iterations. *Journal
  of Research of the National Bureau of Standards* 49, 33–53.
- Fletcher, R. (1976). Conjugate gradient methods for indefinite systems. *Numerical Analysis
  Dundee 1975*, Lecture Notes in Mathematics 506, 73–89.
- Parlett, B. N., Taylor, D. R. and Liu, Z. A. (1985). A look-ahead Lanczos algorithm for
  unsymmetric matrices. *Mathematics of Computation* 44, 105–124.
- van der Vorst, H. A. (1992). Bi-CGSTAB: a fast and smoothly converging variant of Bi-CG for the
  solution of nonsymmetric linear systems. *SIAM Journal on Scientific and Statistical
  Computing* 13, 631–644.
- Saad, Y. (2003). *Iterative Methods for Sparse Linear Systems*, 2nd edition. SIAM, Chapter 7.
- Langville, A. N. and Meyer, C. D. (2006). *Google's PageRank and Beyond*. Princeton University
  Press.
- Sleijpen, G. L. G. and Fokkema, D. R. (1993). BiCGstab(\(\ell\)) for linear equations involving
  unsymmetric matrices with complex spectrum. *Electronic Transactions on Numerical Analysis* 1,
  11–32.
